  "interpretation": [
    "The warmed bottleneck is primarily the actual legacy tjCompress call. It accounts for 89.34% of paired final-minute output-thread CPU, 18.265 ms per update out of 20.445 ms.",
    "Palette/translation takes 0.960 ms and remaining output-thread work 1.220 ms per warmed update. Optimizing only those stages cannot recover a 16.67 ms budget with JPEG cost unchanged.",
    "JPEG CPU rises 6.608 to 18.265 ms per update while mean JPEGcall count remains about 43. Capture continues near 60 Hz and output ends near 30 Hz.",
    "The two fixed JPEG workers are an admissible byte-preserving performance experiment; these serial measurements do not prove its speed, thermal behavior, native latency or quality.",
    "Initial thermal samples include nominal/fair and later serious. This observation does not isolate frequency/core choice or prove thermal causality."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "This attribution applies only to serial output-thread encoding; other threads are deliberately unarmed.",
    "tj_compress measures the exact legacy tjCompress call, including work in that API but excluding buffer setup and protocol output.",
    "tight_palette_translation measures the SendSubrect palette/translation block; it excludes the recursive planner, solid-area search, headers and codec dispatch.",
    "Other CPU is paired whole-output CPU minus the two disjoint measured stages; it includes probe and protocol work.",
    "Exact sums include only sends with complete, same-thread whole/stage pairs. Invalid samples are counted, not replaced with zero.",
    "Thermal states are sampled categories and do not identify temperature, CPU frequency or core assignment."
  ],
